Original commit body from 2802d15730d68db2bb31782651b837f109114e79:

CI's 100% gate covers the whole changed file, so the one-line activation
edit in the previous commit exposed a pre-existing hole: line 230, the
`continue` that skips a trait-REQUIRED method because linkers/type_hierarchy
already emits that relationship at the precise requirement anchor. The
linker's own test file left it uncovered (63 stmts, 1 missed, 98%).

The local scoped run reported 100% because its slice was 211 test files wide
and something in there happened to reach the branch; the dedicated test file
alone does not. File-scoped local coverage can pass on a line no targeted
test covers.

Two tests, because the rule beside it was also unpinned:
  * test_trait_required_method_gets_no_coarse_edge -- the uncovered branch.
    A trait declaring Display::fmt, a Writer::fmt satisfying it (skipped
    here), and an inherent Writer::write (still emitted).
  * test_empty_requirement_set_covers_everything -- an empty requirement set
    means "cover everything", not "cover nothing". Reading it the other way
    drops every edge and resurrects the dead-code false positives this linker
    exists to prevent: absent-versus-empty, one call site further down.

rust_trait_dispatch.py is now 63 stmts, 0 missed.

